+1. `## Evidence & severity`, the scope-creep bullet: state both halves of the consequence, and point at the shared file instead of restating its table. Replace the "under the shared verdict rules a Medium cannot block" clause with: `scope creep can request changes and can never block (see shared/severity.md)`.
+2. `## Evidence & severity`, the `missing` paragraph: add the `line` rule and fix the fallback, per the design. `line` is the first line of the changed hunk where the requirement would have landed, or `0` when there is no such hunk. The fallback is a changed file whose subject area the requirement belongs to, with `line: 0`; if the diff has no such file the finding is not reportable and is dropped. Do not say "point `file` at the spec source itself": two of the four `spec_source.kind` values have no repository path.
+3. `## Evidence & severity`, the `evidence_refs` sentence: keep the canonical meaning from `core/shared/output-schema.md` (the file the evidence came **from**, which for this axis is the spec source), not "where in the repo the requirement is or is not satisfied". The two readings decide whether the out-of-diff tripwire fires, so they cannot both stand.
+4. The `## Output schema` example: `evidence_refs` must point at the spec source, not duplicate `file`. The shared schema says to populate it only when it differs from `file`, and examples are what models copy.
